--8<-- [start:description]

When you search for a plain package name that does not match any package
exactly, `pixi` automatically broadens the search instead of giving up. It
first retries with a prefix match (`<name>*`) and, if that also finds
nothing, with a "contains" match (`*<name>*`). For example, `pixi search ros`
finds no package literally called `ros`, so it falls back to showing every
package starting with `ros` (`ros-core`, `ros-humble-*`, ...).

This only happens for a bare package name. If you provide a glob or extra
constraints (a version, build, channel, ...), your query is used as-is.

--8<-- [end:description]

--8<-- [start:example]

## Examples

```shell
pixi search pixi
# no package is named exactly `ros`, so this falls back to `ros*`
pixi search ros
pixi search --limit 30 "py*"
# search in a different channel and for a specific platform
pixi search -c robostack --platform linux-64 "*plotjuggler*"
# search for a specific version of a package
pixi search "rattler-build<=0.35.4"
pixi search "rattler-build[build_number=h2d22210_0]" --platform linux-64
```

--8<-- [end:example]
